    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the \"reject duplicate telephone with 409\" rule on `POST /api/owners` using OfficeFloor function injection, following the project's conventions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `RequireUniqueTelephone.java` \u2014 a pipeline step that reads the already-normalized owner body (`@Val OwnerFieldsDto`), scans existing owners via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, and throws a checked `DuplicateTelephoneException` if any other owner's normalized telephone matches. It normalizes both sides (strip non-digits) so the comparison is robust even against un-normalized stored/seed data.\n- `DuplicateTelephoneException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it appears in the `throws` clause and is routed to an escalation handler.\n- `DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409 Conflict` with a `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`.\n- `.../escalation/...DuplicateTelephoneException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring change** (`owners.POST.yml`): inserted the `uniqueTelephone` step between `require` (which normalizes the telephone) and `build`/`save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]` so the uniqueness read runs inside the same write transaction as the insert.\n\nThe step order matters: `RequireOwnerFields` normalizes the telephone first (so an invalid telephone still returns 400), then the uniqueness check runs before any persistence, so a duplicate short-circuits with 409 before an owner is created.",
